<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narmada River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Narmada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Narmada is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, flowing about 1,300 kilometres westwards from Amarkantak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> through a rift valley between the Vindhya and Satpura ranges to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>. It has long marked the traditional boundary between northern India and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> halted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> there in the 7th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> regard it as one of the holiest rivers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narmada-river.jpg" alt="Narmada River" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aksveer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narmada_River_Jabalpur_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The greatest victory came under Pulakeshin II, who halted the southward advance of Harsha, ruler of Kannauj, on the line of the <b>Narmada River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 786–790 Dhruva crossed the <b>Narmada</b> and defeated both the Pratihara king Vatsaraja and the Pala king Dharmapala in the Ganga–Yamuna doab.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Within a decade Tailapa had brought most of the old Rashtrakuta lands between the <b>Narmada</b> and the Kaveri under his authority, including Lata in southern Gujarat for a time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pulakeshin II, king of the Chalukyas of Badami, issued the Bijapur-Mumbai grant inscription, which mentions his victory over Harsha of Kanauj on the <b>Narmada</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The rulers of Barwani claimed descent from the Sisodia house of Mewar and held lands in the <b>Narmada</b> valley for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/barwani-state">Barwani State</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom lay on routes leading from the Ganges plain towards the <b>Narmada</b> and the Deccan.</blockquote><a href="/state/chedi">Chedi</a></li></ul></section>
